{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-341781" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $f(x)$ be the $2L$-periodic extension of a function $F(x)$ from $x\\in [-L,L)$ to $x\\in R.$\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.The Fourier series of $f$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({{field-1}})+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({{field-2}}).\n$$\n\nThe $3$rd sine coefficient of the series is\n\n$$\n{b}_{3}={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $F(x)={x}^{5}$ and $L=4,$ the $3$rd sine coefficient becomes\n\n$$\n{b}_{3}={{field-4}}\\,\\cdot \\,{{field-5}}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068894/state.json" "file_sha256" "f3f5cb88e443f7d38ccc5568609fdc38a8a921ea01a96d136d5bb4c57e704bcf"} {"question" "q-341781" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that multiplying the argument of a function by $k\\ne 0$ represents the horizontal stretching of the corresponding graph by a factor of $\\frac{1}{k}.$\n\nNow, let $s=\\frac{\\pi }{L}x.$ Then, since $f(x)$ has a period of $2L,$ the function\n\n$$\ng(s)=f(\\frac{L}{\\pi }s)\n$$\n\nmust have a period of $2L\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}=2\\pi .$ \n\nThe Fourier series of $g(s)$ is\n\n$$\ng(s)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(ns)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(ns),\n$$\n\nwhere:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\ {a}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(ns)\\,\\text{d}s,\\,n\\ge 1 \\\\ {b}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(ns)\\,\\text{d}s,\\,n\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $s=\\frac{\\pi }{L}x$ and using the fact that $g(s)=f(\\frac{L}{\\pi }s),$ we get the Fourier series of $f(x):$\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)\n$$\n\nTherefore, $3$rd sine coefficient of $f(x)$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3s)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(\\frac{L}{\\pi }s)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3s)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(\\frac{L}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}x)\\,\\text{d}(\\frac{\\pi }{L}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{L}x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{L}x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $L=4$ and $f(x)={x}^{5},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {\\int }_{-4}^{4}{x}^{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068894/state.json" "file_sha256" "f3f5cb88e443f7d38ccc5568609fdc38a8a921ea01a96d136d5bb4c57e704bcf"} {"question" "q-341781" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068894/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "97188c44f046ac47f2aeb0650ca4600bc4d2cf9acb58726e1d9f6aefb27d50e6"} {"question" "q-341781" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068894/state.json" "file_sha256" "f3f5cb88e443f7d38ccc5568609fdc38a8a921ea01a96d136d5bb4c57e704bcf"} {"question" "q-341781" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{L}{n\\pi }x